{
  "title": "iOS UI CI: build the server and the app on parallel runners",
  "description": "The iOS UI Tests job takes about 23 minutes on one macos-latest runner, and the steps run one after another: about 5.5 min building the release server, about 4 min building the debug CLI, and about 10 min in 'Run UI tests', of which roughly 6.5 is the xcodebuild app build and 3.5 the tests themselves (run on PR #748). \
The repo is public, so standard GitHub-hosted runners, macOS included, cost nothing. \
Larger runners (macos-*-large/xlarge) are billed even for public repos, so they are out. \
Split the work across parallel standard runners instead: one job builds the server and CLI, one runs `xcodebuild build-for-testing` for the app, and a final job downloads both as artifacts and runs `xcodebuild test-without-building` against the seeded server. \
Wall-clock time should drop to about the longest build plus the test run. \
Optionally, shard the UI tests one per job (`-only-testing:RamekinUITests/RecipeFlowTests/<test>`) so each gets a fresh simulator. \
The SpringBoard stall in run 37082683506 started about 7 minutes into a single simulator session.",
